DRAKE type-definition template  (.dlt = definition language template)
Hand-authoring reference until the .drake -> JSON parser exists.
Each template line shows the drake syntax on the left and, after ->, the JSON
TypeDefinition element it emits.

NOTATION
  keyword          a literal drake keyword
  *keyword         PRESENCE-SIGNIFICANT optional section — may be omitted, but its presence-vs-absence
                   carries meaning: writing it (even empty) is a positive statement; omitting it makes
                   the facet absent. present-empty != absent.
  ~keyword         ELIDABLE field — present-empty == absent (emptiness carries no information; only
                   content matters). Empty is omitted from JSON. Combine: *~keyword (optional AND elidable).
  <fill-in>        a name or value you supply
  *<fill-in>       an optional operand
  a|b|c            choose one
  ...              the preceding line repeats
  indentation      shows nesting to the READER only — never to the parser (see BRACKETS)
  [ ] { } ( )      literal drake Seq / Set / tuple-or-type-application
  ->               "emits"; a trailing [] on the right = a JSON array (one entry per line)
  { a, b }         a JSON object with those fields; kind=X sets the kind discriminator

FILL-INS
  <name>           an identifier
  <type>           a type name
  <T>              a type parameter: <name>, upper-bounded as <name> <: <bound>, or
                   context-bounded as <name>: <bound> (e.g. T: Decoder — carried verbatim)
  <bound>          the type after <: (e.g. Product, DracoType)
  <value-type>     a type expression (see VALUE-TYPES)
  <value>          an expression or "quoted literal" (see EXPRESSIONS)
  <default-value>  a parameter default
  <bool-value>     a Boolean expression
  <unit-value>     a side-effecting statement (Unit)
  <wire-key>       the JSON key a field encodes to / decodes from (defaults to <name>)
  <pkg-seq>        a space-separated package path -> a namePackage array

VALUE-TYPES  (drake type-expression -> JSON valueType)
  TEXT (2026-09-22): `draco.drake.Text` is draco-owned immutable Unicode text. Equality is exact,
  with no implicit Unicode normalization. The empty literal is text; `++` concatenates text,
  and `join(separator, texts)` inserts the separator between members, yielding empty text for
  an empty sequence and the member unchanged for a singleton. No character indexing, length
  unit, locale comparison, normalization or malformed-encoding policy is introduced here.
  The Scala target realizes a Text-typed value as `String`, not a wrapper instance. The generated
  Text companion/trait describe the type, as target-backed capability descriptors do; they are
  not the runtime representation of a Text value. References use the qualified identity
  `draco.drake.Text` in this first increment, including within compound type forms. A bare `Text`
  is not globally reserved or rewritten. Nominal is the first consumer: `Primal(draco.drake.Text)`.
  Surface, DomainLine, DomainLineOf and Emission also name Text in their text inputs and results.
  Remaining String slots are unmigrated; other target realizations are not implemented yet.

  THE FIELD IS A JSON NODE (2026-09-16), on the same terms as a TypeElement's `value`: a STRING is the
  type's authored text, host-opaque, and is everything the corpus holds today; an OBJECT will be a
  TYPE-FORM TREE — Atomic (a string leaf), Objective `{"(,)": [...]}`, Parametric `{"()": [F, A ...]}`,
  Morphic `{"->": [S, T]}` binary and RIGHT-associative (Haskell's: A -> B -> C is A -> (B -> C)), the
  bounds `{"<:": [parameter, bound]}` / `{">:": [parameter, bound]}` parameter first. THE PARSER TREES
  THE FOUR FORMS (2026-09-17): every value-type slot parses to a tree, read outside-in — a top-level
  arrow first (grouping right), then a bound, then the bracket forms, then an application, else Atomic —
  and the emitter spells a tree back from TypeForm's neutral rendering, a top-level arrow parenthesized
  in the name-type-value slot. The rows below are read as the SURFACE spelling of each form; their
  right-hand column is the host text a STRING leaf still carries, which after the corpus
  re-canonicalizes is only `mut {T}` (`mut` belongs to the element — agreed 2026-09-13, not yet built —
  so its type stays host text until it does).
  [<T>]            {"[]": [<T>]}       the three COLLECTION rows are neutral in the JSON (2026-09-18):
  {<T>}            {"{}": [<T>]}       the bracket is the node key, at one arity a set and at two a
  {<K>, <V>}       {"{}": [<K>, <V>]}  map; Seq / Set / Map are spelled by the Scala target alone
  [<T>]+           mutable.Buffer[<T>]  the MUTABLE collections (2026-09-20, Dev): the bracket pair names the
  {<T>}+           mutable.Set[<T>]     collection and a trailing `+` its mutable version, WHEREVER a type is
  {<K>, <V>}+      mutable.Map[<K>, <V>] written — {"[]+": [T]}, {"{}+": [...]} in the JSON. COLLECTION BRACKETS
                                       ONLY: `()+` is left out, reserved for mutable TREES (mixed mutable and
                                       immutable tree types and tree instance values — Dev's idea, later).
                                       `fix` / `mut` say nothing about the container: they are the NAME's val /
                                       var alone. An empty `[]` / `{}` in a `+` slot is the mutable empty. The
                                       `+` grants ONE operation, `add(xs, x)` (SYMBOLS). `mut {T}` host text
                                       RETIRED with this row (Accumulator was its last use).
  <F>(<A> ...)     <F>[<A> ...]        type application
  (<A>, <B>)       (<A>, <B>)          tuple, unchanged
  <A> -> <B>       <A> => <B>          function arrow — the SAME token drake uses for a "->" VALUE node
                                       (see EXPRESSIONS). One arrow in the language, not one per position.
  <Name>           <Name>              plain type, unchanged
  THE BRACE FAMILY is ONE bracket at two arities: {<T>} a set, {<K>, <V>} a map — a map being a set of
  pairs is why the two share a bracket rather than each taking its own.
  MOST ROWS ARE SURFACE SUBSTITUTIONS, not neutral terms: the JSON still holds a Scala type string
  (Seq, mutable.Set, =>), and drake merely spells it its own way. Giving the TERM to draco — so that a
  drake type name resolves through draco's own dictionary even when it happens to coincide with the host's
  spelling — is the wrap-type direction, one tier below draco.rete's wrapping of host library types.
  Coincidence of spelling is not identity of term.
  THE COLLECTION ROWS ARE ACROSS (2026-09-18; the map row was first, 0acf2da). Their JSON *is* the drake
  form — `{"[]": [T]}`, `{"{}": [T]}`, `{"{}": [K, V]}` — and `Seq[T]` / `Set[T]` / `Map[K, V]` are produced
  by the Scala target alone, in the one function that spells a tree for it. Between 2026-09-16 and
  2026-09-18 the parser carried them as Seq / Set / Map APPLICATIONS, a host head in the normative corpus
  that drake itself never wrote; Dev's reading of Dictionary's `{K, V1}` slot corrected it. The same holds
  in VALUE position: `[]` and `{}` are `{"[]": []}` / `{"{}": []}`, a sequence or set literal with its
  elements, and each target spells the empty one (`Seq.empty`); a NON-EMPTY literal parses too since
  2026-09-20 — `[name]`, `[x, y]`, `{a, b}`: one bracket group with nothing before it, members read as
  values (TypeName's `namePackage ++ [name]` is the first use). The rows that still put the HOST's
  spelling in the corpus are the leaves (String, Int, Json, URI …) and the heads with no drake form yet
  — none of the HEADS is left. JAVA.UTIL.LIST WENT FIFTH (2026-09-20): the collectors an actor's caller hands
  in and a rule appends to (Draco's problems, Emitter's received, the scenario's received, aerial's consumed)
  are "a sequenced values container for post hoc sorting into an indexed table" (Dev) — not a consumer — and
  a container's mutability must be in its TYPE, because a `mut` on the caller's element is invisible inside
  the callee's `par`: so `[Problem]+`, `loc received [Emission]+`, `add(received, emission)`. The leaves
  (String, Int, Json, URI, the evrete and pekko types) remain the host's spellings.
  LAZYLIST WENT FOURTH (2026-09-20, Dev: "`[T]` is the lazy sequence, unboundedness in the value"): drake
  evaluates by need, so `[T]` IS Haskell's list and no second sequence form exists; Primes' five
  `LazyList(Int)` slots are `[Int]`. The residue is the ScalaTarget's alone — its Seq is strict — and it is
  the two ways a definition BUILDS a sequence lazily, `progression` and `cons` (SYMBOLS), spelled with
  LazyList, whose values are Seqs, so head / tail / filter / take on a `[T]` stay lazy at run time.
  CONSUMER WENT THIRD (2026-09-20, Dev: "I would prefer that Consumer not appear in drake or json as part
  of the type definition"): Rule / RuleType declare `pattern (Knowledge -> Unit)` and `action (RhsContext
  -> Unit)` — both arguments draco.rete wrap types, so the slot is fully draco-spelled — and a rule's
  pattern is APPLIED, `pattern(knowledge)`. Java's Consumer was there because Evrete is Java and its
  `execute` takes java.util.function.Consumer; a Scala function VALUE is refused at that call and a lambda
  LITERAL converts, so the ScalaTarget writes `execute (action(_))` at that one call and names the
  interface nowhere else. ACTORREF WENT SECOND (2026-09-18, Dev: "a consumer of M, the assembly keeps identity"): a parameter
  typed by the host's `ActorRef(M)` is `(M -> Unit)` — drake's own arrow, parenthesized in the
  name-type-value slot — and sending is APPLICATION, `consumer(report)`. The ASSEMBLY keeps the host's refs
  and converts each at its boundary (`ref ! _`) when it hands a member its arguments; a member only ever
  applies a function. The term "ActorRef" is provisional — Pekko's name for the address of a typed actor's
  message queue — until Pekko is integrated through drake definitions with ScalaTarget implementations.
  OPTION WENT FIRST (2026-09-18): the loader path — DefinitionPath.source, TypeLoader's
  readDefinition / loadFromResource / tryLoad — declares `Presence(T)`, converts ONCE at its host boundary
  with the `presence` symbol, and chains with `fold`. Dictionary.get keeps Option: it overrides the
  host's Map.get, whose result the host fixes, the same way its parameter is `now`.

EXPRESSIONS  (value expressions — normative JSON tree vs drake surface)
  A <value> emits to JSON in one of two forms; the value's own JSON structure IS the
  classification (no flag, no version field):
    string             HOST-OPAQUE source text, carried verbatim (the legacy/default form).
                       A bare string is source text / a symbol reference; a string literal
                       keeps its embedded quotes ("\"ctx\""). Json null == absent == "".
    { op: [operands] } a structured expression TREE: a single-key object applying the
                       operator symbol op to its operands, each operand itself an
                       expression (string leaf or nested tree).
  OPERATORS — the op key is a SYMBOL REFERENCE, to be resolved against declared symbols:
    "."   path join / selection; FLAT VARIADIC — associative operators flatten
          ({".": ["org","evrete","api","RhsContext"]}), non-associative stay binary
    "->"  function/type arrow — HASKELL FORM; binary, right-associative at the surface;
          ScalaTarget renders " => " (in a String-typed slot the quoted rendering carries
          the target spelling: {"->": [RhsContext-path, "Unit"]} -> "org.evrete.api.RhsContext => Unit")
    INFIX OPERATORS — every declared infix operator is a node key, operands joined with the spaced
          operator on either surface ({"==": [{"*": ["i1","i2"]}, "i3"]} renders "i1 * i2 == i3").
          The table, and its FIXITY (the parser's, 2026-09-21 — Haskell's Prelude, since drake takes
          Haskell's semantics; a tree is already associated, so fixity is a surface concern only):
            7  *  /  %        left
            6  +  -           left
            5  ++             right, FLAT VARIADIC (one node holds the whole run)
            4  == != < <= > >=   none (a chain `a == b == c` is an error at parse, as in Haskell)
            3  &&             right, FLAT VARIADIC
            2  ||             right, FLAT VARIADIC
            0  ->             right — BELOW every other row, so a lambda body takes everything to its
                              right (Dev, 2026-09-21: the arrow's fixity is what makes a lambda's scope
                              unambiguous). `->` is also the function/type arrow node (below).
    "()"  application — first operand applied to the rest; a literal argument keeps its
          embedded quotes ({"()": [{".": ["Rule","knowledgeService","newKnowledge"]}, "\"Primes\""]}
          renders Rule.knowledgeService.newKnowledge("Primes")). drake surface f(a, b) — see
          APPLICATION SURFACE; a named argument is the "=" node below it, spelled name:value.
    "\"   lambda — HASKELL FORM: leading operands are the parameters, last is the body.
          drake surface \e a -> body ; ScalaTarget renders (e, a) => body (bare param when single).
          The tree carries no arrow token — structure separates params from body.
    (no "if" node: a CONDITIONAL is `ifThenElse` on a Presence — `guard(c).ifThenElse(t, e)`, a call
          like any other, both branches by need. The Haskell-form node Value once carried retired
          2026-09-21 with its last two uses.)
    "++"  CONCATENATION — HASKELL FORM; FLAT VARIADIC (associative): one node holds every piece in
          order, string literals interleaved with names and applications — the neutral form of a
          SUBSTITUTION STRING ({"++": ["\"domain \"", {"()": ["packagePart","package"]}, "\" \"", "name"]}).
          drake surface a ++ b ++ c ; ScalaTarget renders " ++ " (tighter than +, which coerces any
          right operand to text). The first operator the parser treed (2026-09-10), reserved until the
          operator layer landed (2026-09-21); now one row of the fixity table like any other.
  DECLARED NAMES (SYMBOLS) — a name applied on the application surface whose spelling is per target,
    the primitives a substitution string needs beyond `++`. Each is declared here before use, and
    each target renders it (DracoGenerator.scalaSymbols today; GenScala's transform types in time):
    presence <x>       the host's OPTIONAL value as a Presence(T) — the ONE conversion at a host boundary
                       (2026-09-18): `presence(found.headOption)`. ScalaTarget renders a match into
                       Present / Absent, typed by the declared result. Chaining past the boundary is
                       `fold`, never a host combinator: `source(p).fold(Absent(), readDefinition)`.
    progression <start> <step>   the UNBOUNDED arithmetic sequence — Haskell's [start, start+step ..] — as a
                       `[T]` (2026-09-20). ScalaTarget: `LazyList.from(start, step)`, a Seq that is lazy.
    cons <x> <xs>      Haskell's `x : xs`, the tail by need (2026-09-20). ScalaTarget: `LazyList.cons(x,
                       LazyList.from(xs))` — from returns a LazyList unchanged and wraps any other Seq
                       lazily, so a recursive definition (Primes' sieve) terminates.
    guard <b>          a truth as a PRESENCE OF NOTHING — Haskell's guard (2026-09-20, Dev: "a presence of
                       nothing, bridged by guard"): `Present(())` when b holds, `Absent` when not, a
                       Presence(Unit). THE bridge from a host Boolean to `ifThenElse`, which dispatches on
                       a Presence: `guard(p).ifThenElse(t, e)`. ScalaTarget: an `if` into Present[Unit] /
                       Absent[Unit]. The predicate itself stays host text behind it, as the circe read
                       stays behind `presence`.
    add <xs> <x>       IN-PLACE add to a `+` collection (2026-09-20) — `add(received, emission)`. ScalaTarget:
                       `xs += x`, the same call on a Buffer and a mutable Set. Not `append`, which is
                       Haskell's `++`, concatenation, and not in place.
    join  <sep> <xs>   Haskell's intercalate, separator FIRST — `join(" ", _package)`; a compound xs (a `++`
                       run) is parenthesized by the ScalaTarget: `(namePackage ++ Seq(name)).mkString(".")`;
                       ScalaTarget renders xs.mkString(sep). Chosen over `intercalate` (obscure) and
                       `splice` (means insertion in JS/Perl) as the name every target's reader knows;
                       chosen over an invented infix (`+|+`) because a name carries its meaning in and
                       costs no reservation or fixity entry. (2026-09-11)
    bracket <acquire> <release> <use>   Haskell's Control.Exception.bracket (2026-09-22): acquire a resource, hand
                       it to `use`, and run `release` on it whatever `use` does — the RESOURCE SCOPE as application,
                       no block on the surface. `use` and `release` are lambdas over the resource. ScalaTarget spells
                       the block an author writes, the resource bound to `use`'s parameter: `{ val s = acquire; try
                       <use body> finally <release body> }` (a release parameter named otherwise is bound inside the
                       finally). The one use: TypeLoader.readDefinition, `presence(bracket(scala.io.Source.fromURL(url),
                       \s -> s.close(), \s -> io.circe.parser.parse(s.mkString).flatMap(_.as[TypeDefinition]).toOption))`.
                       Chosen because the Generator can spell it for every target and it adds no host name to drake
                       (Dev, 2026-09-22).
    (further operators arrive with corpus conversion, each declared before use)
  HASKELL-FORM SURFACE — drake adopts Haskell's syntactic form for the lambda (\p1 p2 -> body); the
  tree is form-neutral and each generator transform (GenScala, GenDrake, …) renders its own spelling
  (ScalaTarget shown above). Host-bound vocabulary inside a construct (null, asInstanceOf, hcursor)
  stays verbatim — form is drake's, atoms may be the target's. Exemplar: Value.drake's dyn value
  (lambdas over `guard(…).ifThenElse(…)`).
  FIXITY lives ONLY at the drake surface: a JSON tree is already associated, so precedence
  and associativity are pure parse/print concerns. THE OPERATOR LAYER (parser, 2026-09-21): a
  value's token run is split FLAT at every operator in the table above and reassociated by
  fixity (precedence climbing, Haskell's resolution). A lambda `\p1 p2 -> body` is read in the
  same pass — a token opening with `\` in operand position takes every token after it: the
  parameters up to the arrow, the body as an expression — so it is always the LAST operand of
  its run. THE WHOLE-RUN RULE: a run trees only when every operand is ONE token (a name, a
  literal, a call, a tuple, a collection literal, a parenthesized group) or the lambda; a run
  with a several-token operand — a host `if`, a `new`, a block, a typed binder outside a
  lambda — is a form the parser does not read, and treeing around it would hand the generator
  a tree with the WRONG SCOPE, so the whole run stays the raw leaf it was and the loss report
  measures it. A PARENTHESIZED GROUP holding one expression the parser reads DISSOLVES into
  that tree (2026-09-21): the pair is not information once the tree is associated, and each
  renderer writes back the MINIMAL pair by its own target's fixity — the emitter by this table,
  the ScalaTarget by Scala's (where `++` ranks with `+` and every operator groups left, so
  `xs ++ (n + 1)` keeps its pair in Scala and loses it in drake) — a lambda or conditional
  as an operand taking a pair wherever the surface could not read it whole. A group the parser
  reads as a LEAF keeps its parentheses (`(x: T)`, a host `if`). Still ahead of the grammar
  sketched here: application by juxtaposition, `(op)` as a value, `name` infix.
  RENDERING (procedural Generator, current): Generator.expression renders a tree back to
  its Scala surface — "." dot-joins, every infix operator joins with its spaced symbol, "->"
  joins with " => ", "()" applies, "\" renders its Scala spelling — with the
  MINIMAL parentheses by Scala's own precedence (2026-09-21). A tree in a String-typed slot
  renders as a string literal: the slot's runtime type is String, so the tree denotes its
  surface text (TypeElement's own valueType field is the exemplar). TypeElement.value is
  Json; a string value passes through expression() verbatim, so the whole legacy corpus is
  unchanged. isString = host-opaque tail (measured, not hidden); isObject = drake-native.

APPLICATION SURFACE  (how a "()" tree is written in a value position — the CALL, one token)
  <fn>(<arg>, <arg> ...)             -> { "()": [ <fn> , <arg> ... ] }
    <value>                          a POSITIONAL argument
    <name>:<value>                   a NAMED argument -> { "=": [ <name> , <value> ] }
  Positional arguments come FIRST, then named ones; each parameter is named at most once (an error at
  parse); a parameter not given takes the DEFAULT its declaration carries (`par step Int 1`). The tree
  keeps "=" as the named-argument node — a symbol reference, not a surface marker — and the surface
  spells the colon (agreed 2026-09-13; landed 2026-09-16).
  ONE TOKEN. A bracketed group glues to the word before it and whitespace INSIDE a group never ends a
  token (NOTATION), so a call is a single token however many lines its arguments are spread over, and it
  BOUNDS ITSELF: nothing after the `)` can be claimed by it, and nothing inside it needs a bracket. That
  is what retired the multi-line form this section used to describe — `<fn> parameters` with `par` lines,
  `par = <name>` for a named argument, `.member parameters` chain continuations, and `[ ]` around an
  argument or a `++` operand that was itself an application. Every one of those existed to bound a list
  that could not bound itself; the parentheses do it. `parameters` / `par` remain the DECLARATION words
  (a factory's or a dyn's parameter list) and never appear in a call.
  INSIDE THE PARENTHESES the reserved words are ordinary text. A top-level value slot is bounded by the
  next reserved keyword because nothing else can bound it; an argument is bounded by its commas and the
  closing `)`, so `LazyList.from(start, step)` applies to a parameter named `start`, and a lambda binder
  may be called `domain`. What IS read inside is the operator layer (EXPRESSIONS): operators by
  fixity, and a lambda, exactly as at the top level.
  HOW THE TOKEN IS TREED. From the end: a token ending in a `( )` group applies what precedes the group
  to what the group holds, split at depth-0 commas (string literals skipped whole); each argument is
  read as a value in its own right, so a nested call trees and a text no grammar covers (`primes.last -
  1` is now a `-` tree; `Seq(msg): _*` and a host `if` stay raw leaves with their spacing intact). What precedes the group
  is a PATH split at depth-0 dots, and the last element that is itself a call closes the receiver:
      cursor.get[Double]("latitude").getOrElse(0.0)
        -> { "()": [ { ".": [ { "()": [ "cursor.get[Double]", "\"latitude\"" ] }, "getOrElse" ] }, "0.0" ] }
  which is the same nested spine the retired chain lines spelled one call per line — a chain is not a
  construct, it is a call whose function is a selection on a call. A path with no call in it stays the
  one string it was written as (`0.0` included). A group with NOTHING before it is a TUPLE when it holds a
  depth-0 comma — `("type", f("LOCATION"))` -> { "(,)": [ ... ] } — and otherwise a PARENTHESIZED
  SUB-EXPRESSION, which dissolves into its tree when the parser reads one and stays an opaque leaf
  with its pair when it does not (`(x)` is never a one-tuple; see EXPRESSIONS). The emitter writes the flat form back (`f(a, b)`, `, ` between
  arguments, `name:value`), so a value is ONE LINE on the surface in every form.
  NO HEAD ELISION. A construction of the declared value type spells the head like any other call —
  `fix report LocationReport LocationReport(typeDefinition:td, json:payload)` — because `(…)` alone is a
  tuple. The elision that dropped the head before `parameters` went with that form.
  CONCATENATION — a `++` sequence in a value position is one flat tree, and each operand is a LEAF (a
  literal or a name) or a CALL, which is one token and so needs nothing to bound it:
      fix value String "domain " ++ join(" ", _package) ++ " " ++ _name
  `++` is one row of the fixity table (it was a reserved token from 2026-09-10 to 2026-09-21): a
  value slot runs to the next keyword and the operators inside it are read by fixity, at the top level
  and inside a call's parentheses alike, so `f("a" ++ b)` is f applied to the concatenation, and a
  concatenation may be an argument or a tuple member.

REFERENCES  (how `from` and `modules` name another type)
  <Name> *(<A> ...)                  BARE — resolves in the REFERRING TYPE'S OWN package
  <pkg-seq> <Name> *(<A> ...)        QUALIFIED — lower-case package words, then the name
  A same-package reference's package is not information: it is the package we are already in, so it
  is inferred rather than spelled (see INFERENCE). Measured over the corpus when the rule landed:
  67 of 112 references are same-package, 27 point at draco, 17 elsewhere.
  THE SEQUENCE NEEDS NO SEPARATOR. Case bounds each member and therefore the whole run: lower-case
  words are package, the first upper-case token ends the reference, and the next lower-case word
  begins the following one — `from draco Dictionary(K, V) draco DracoType` is two references, and
  `from Unit draco Primal(T)` is a bare one followed by a qualified one. Same rule that already
  bounds `extensible org apache pekko actor typed ExtensibleBehavior(T)`.
  A FOREIGN parent — one outside every draco domain — is not a reference at all but a TYPE EXPRESSION,
  `from {K, V}` (Dictionary), and the carrier holds it as the type-form node it is (2026-09-20:
  `derivation` is `[Json]`, a TypeName for a draco parent, a form for a foreign one; `DracoAspect.parents`
  reads the names). An EMPTY package therefore says nothing about foreignness: it is the NAMELESS
  DOMAIN, draco's default package, whose identity is the empty path — no name, no companion, nothing
  permanent may refer to it; `.drake` (the one line `domain`) and `.json` at the resource root anchor it.
  `domain` / `super` / `extensible` are ALWAYS qualified — each names something outside the type
  being declared, so there is no own-package for them to be inferred against. `messageType` is NOT
  one of these: an actor's message type is an ordinary type reference and follows the bare/qualified
  rule above.
  FOREIGN — a reference with NO package at all names a type outside every draco domain (every draco
  type is declared inside one, so an absent package says the referent is not draco's). It is spelled
  as a TYPE EXPRESSION, not as a name: Dictionary's map parent reads `from {K, V}`, the same notation
  its own `kvMap` element uses. The operator carries what the name used to, so there is no package to
  lose and no bare name to resolve — which is what closed the last round-trip loss (GitHub #51).
  RESIDUAL: a foreign type with no operator to spell it would still read as a bare name, and so still
  come back owning the referring package. The corpus has none; DrakeParseTest's loss report would name
  one if it arrived.

CONVENTIONS
  factory          takes no name when it constructs the enclosing type <name> *(<T>, ...) — that is the default
                   valueType and is inferable, so it is elided. `factory <value-type>` when it constructs
                   something ELSE, which is not reconstructable: the live case is `factory ActorType`, the
                   actor-minting factory, whose value-type is what tells the target to mint an actor rather
                   than an instance of the type.
  [] | {}          in the VALUE position of a name-valueType-value line: the empty-collection default —
                   [] = Seq.empty, {} = Set.empty. Neutral since 2026-09-18 — {"[]": []} / {"{}": []} in the JSON; before, the value carried the host
                   expression (the {".": ["Seq","empty"]} tree, or a legacy "Seq()" string).
  loc <name> <value-type> <value>   a factory/method LOCAL binding -> { kind=Local }: emits a strict
                   `val <name>: <value-type> = <value>`, NOT a declared element (no trait member, no
                   override/lazy). Contrast `fix` in a factory body -> `override lazy val` (a declared element).
                   Use for construction-local intermediates (ContentSink.sinkPath, SourceContent.sourceURI).
  par _<name>      the leading _ is part of the JSON parameter name; a universal guard against a
                   parameter colliding with a same-named body element during mapping
  (<T>, ...)       type parameters -> typeName.typeParameters[], one entry per param, verbatim with any bound
                   — each entry a JSON NODE since 2026-09-17 on the value-type terms (VALUE-TYPES), and THE PARSER
                   BUILDS THE TREE for every header parameter and reference argument: `T` an Atomic string,
                   `S <: DomainType` the bound leaf {"<:": ["S", "DomainType"]}, `(S, T)` an Objective:
                     (T <: Product)                    -> [ "T <: Product" ]
                     (S <: DracoType, T <: DracoType)  -> [ "S <: DracoType", "T <: DracoType" ]

DYN-WITH-BODY  (a Dynamic element/global carrying parameters and a body — i.e. a method)
  A leaf dyn stays one line: `dyn <name> <value-type> *<value>` — the trailing value, when present, IS the
  method's result expression (a no-param one-liner: `dyn iterator Iterator((K, V)) kvMap.iterator`).
  Adding parameters or a body makes it a block method, and a block method BRACKETS ITS OWN BODY (see BRACKETS):
  dyn <name> <value-type> [                -> { kind=Dynamic , name , valueType , parameters[] , body[] }
                                              ( `value` is carried by the LEAF form only )
    ~parameters                            -> parameters[]   ( same block shape as factory.parameters )
      par _<name> <value-type> *<default>  -> { kind=Parameter , ... }
    ~body                                  -> body[]   ( same block shape as factory.body; the `body` head follows
                                                         `parameters`, as originally conceived )
      *~loc|fix|mut <name> <value-type> <value>   -> body[] BodyElement
      *~mon <unit-value>                   -> body[] { kind=Monadic , value }
      *~case *<name> <value-type> [ ... ]  -> body[] { kind=Case }   ( see CASE-BRANCH )
      *~fix value <value-type> <expr>      -> body[] { kind=Fixed , name="value" }   ( THE RESULT — see below )
  ]                                        ( the `[` is what tells a leaf dyn from a block one )
  THE RESULT IS THE BODY ELEMENT NAMED `value` (2026-09-13). Every TypeElement is a Primal(Json) and so
  carries a `value`; a factory body already sets the instance's `value` with `fix value <type> <expr>`
  (draketarget/DomainLine), and a dyn body does the same for the method's result. It is found BY NAME,
  not by position — nothing has to be last — and its value-type must agree with the dyn's (a lint once
  value types are structured). A body with no `value` element is a Unit method: its statements are all
  effects. A block dyn carries NO value field of its own. The `= <expr>` result marker that once made
  that field visible is RETIRED: it was a body-syntax exception for one kind, where `dyn` was only ever
  meant to add polymorphism (an overridable member), never a different body syntax. The body syntax is
  now the same for a factory and a dyn; that fix/mut may take parameters and a body on the same terms
  is agreed and deferred.
  A method type-parameter rides the name via the ( ) convention: `dyn updated(V1 >: V) ...` -> name "updated[V1 >: V]".
  Valid wherever an element list is: elements, factory body, globalElements.

CASE-BRANCH  (a Case element: an ordered branch on which SIBLING SUBTYPE of a scrutinee arrived)
  First-match-wins. Admitted in an actor's `message` and in a dyn-with-body — the second is where
  the projection domains dispatch over TypeElement's kinds, and where TypeName.equals lands.
  It is NOT the rule LHS and shares no type with it: a rule `pattern` has NO scrutinee (it quantifies
  over working memory) and is unordered; a case has exactly one and is ordered. `Pattern` carries
  `conditions`; a case carries none.
  case *<name> <value-type> [            -> body[] { kind=Case , name , valueType , body[] }
    ( statements sit DIRECTLY under the case — the same admits as a dyn-with-body )
    *~loc|fix|mut <name> <value-type> <value>  -> body[] BodyElement   ( STATEMENTS ONLY )
    *~mon <unit-value>                   -> body[] { kind=Monadic , value }
    *~fix value <value-type> <expr>      -> body[] { kind=Fixed , name="value" }   ( the RESULT, found by name — exactly as in a dyn-with-body )
  ]
  BINDER OPTIONAL — `case a AlarmSignal [ ... ]` binds the narrowed value, `case Fixed [ ... ]` does not
  (name absent == ""). The UNBOUND form is the common one wherever a branch dispatches without reaching
  into the value: Drake.keyword is 8-of-9 unbound. Both project — Scala `case a: T =>` / `case _: T =>`,
  Haskell `T a ->` / `T _ ->`.
  STATEMENTS-OR-RESULT, as in a dyn-with-body: an actor branch is all effects and carries no `value` element;
  a projection branch carries one and usually nothing else. ONE kind, both shapes.
  THE DEFAULT BRANCH is a branch with NO TYPE, and CASE alone tells the four forms apart — the same rule
  that already bounds a reference (REFERENCES: lower-case is a package word, the first upper-case token
  ends the run). Nothing else is consulted:
    case a AlarmSignal [ ... ]   binder + type          case e [ ... ]   default, bound
    case AlarmSignal [ ... ]     type, unbound          case [ ... ]     default, unbound
  WHEN A DEFAULT IS NEEDED follows from STATEMENTS-OR-RESULT, and the two shapes differ:
    effects  — none. A scrutinee matching no branch is a no-op and the target writes its own catch-all.
    result   — required, unless the branches are exhaustive over the scrutinee's subtypes (which the
               definition graph knows). Drake.keyword lists 8 of TypeElement's 11 kinds and ends in an
               error default; without a default branch that shape has no spelling.
  NOT AUTHORED, because reconstructable: the scrutinee NAME — it is the enclosing construct's subject.
  In an actor's `message` that is the message; in a dyn-with-body it is the dyn's SINGLE parameter, and
  a dyn that carries cases and any other number of parameters is an error, not a guess. A branch type is
  expected to derive the scrutinee's type; that is a lint, not a syntax rule, and a default branch is
  exempt from it by construction.
  CONSECUTIVE CASES ARE ONE DISPATCH. A run of case elements in a body projects as one match on the
  scrutinee; a statement after the run belongs to the enclosing block (BRACKETS). Two runs separated by
  a statement are two dispatches on the same scrutinee.
  OPTIONAL WHEREVER ADMITTED: a `message` whose body is plain statements is an actor that does not
  dispatch — 13 of the corpus's 14 actors. A single whole-type branch (`case ashSap AshSap [ ... ]`) is
  legal, and is how a message is given an authored name rather than the target's supplied one.

EVALUATION  (lazy by default, `now` to override — Haskell's stance, agreed 2026-09-17)
  Every binding a body evaluates (fix, loc), every parameter and every method is LAZY: CALL-BY-NEED, evaluated
  at most once, on first use, and never if unused. A target whose default is eager renders the lazy forms
  as its deferred ones — ScalaTarget: a body binding is `lazy val`; a parameter is by-name `_x: => T` bound
  to `lazy val x = _x` on entry (by-name alone would be call-by-name, re-evaluated per use). `mut` has no
  lazy form and is strict by nature. A field a factory body sets stays the trait's `lazy val` (no strict val
  may override it). Effects (`mon`) run in order as statements; a lazy binding runs when first read, which is
  what lets `ifThenElse` as dispatch on Presence terminate a recursion — its branches are never both forced.
    now <member>                     -> { …, now: true }   the STRICTNESS OVERRIDE (Haskell's `!`), a modifier
                                        before the member's own keyword: `now loc x Int f(n)`, `now par n Int`.
                                        `now` is reserved; absent means lazy. It rides the element (TypeElement.now,
                                        Boolean, elided when false) and applies to fix / mut / loc / par / dyn.
  LANDED IN THREE STEPS, smallest semantic risk first, the corpus needing `now` nowhere (measured 2026-09-17):
    1. body bindings -> lazy val, with `now` itself            (2026-09-17)
    2. method parameters -> by-name + lazy val prelude          (2026-09-18; `now par other Any` on
                                                                 TypeName.equals, the host's by-value signature)
    3. factory parameters -> by-name                            (2026-09-18; the factory BODY is the prelude:
                                                                 a bodiless factory binds `override lazy val
                                                                 x = _x` once, a bodied one reads `_x` inside
                                                                 the lazy members it authors)
    4. actor-minting factory parameters -> by-name              (2026-09-18; `factory ActorType` is a factory:
                                                                 the instance binds `lazy val x = _x` once, the
                                                                 actions read the bare name — the same prelude)

BRACKETS  (a construct delimits ITSELF; whitespace is insignificant throughout)
  A list-block (elements, parameters, body, globalElements, variables, conditions, modules, types) is
  written head-then-members. Whitespace — spaces, newlines, indentation — is INSIGNIFICANT, purely for
  readability: member values parse as expressions, and the reserved section/member keywords bound each member.
  Three rules, each LOCAL — nothing needs look-ahead, and nothing consults layout:
    NAME list      modules / types: members carry no keyword, so nothing else can bound them  -> [ ] ALWAYS
    KEYWORD block  every other list-block: bounded by the member keywords it ADMITS            -> NO brackets
    OPENER         a member that opens its own sub-block brackets ITSELF                       -> [ ] ALWAYS
  TWO OPENERS, one rule: a dyn-with-body (which opens parameters and a body) and a CASE-BRANCH (which
  opens its own statements). In the first the `[` also tells the leaf form from the block one: a leaf dyn
  opens nothing and so brackets nothing. A case has NO leaf form — it always opens a body and always
  brackets it, for the reason the rule exists: the statement after the last branch belongs to the
  enclosing block, and whitespace cannot say so. There WERE three: an argument whose value was an
  application was the third (GitHub #60), until the call became one token (APPLICATION SURFACE) and its
  parentheses bounded it — a construct that delimits itself needs no bracket added.
  ADMITS is what ends a keyword block: `parameters` takes only `par`, so a `loc` after it is the enclosing
  dyn's first statement, not a parameter; `elements` / `body` / `globals` take fix|mut|dyn|loc.
  THE OPENER BRACKETS, NOT THE CONTAINER.  Bracketing the container marks where the CONTAINER ends, but the
  overlap that needs resolving is between an opener's last sub-block and the container's NEXT member — so a
  fully container-bracketed corpus can still be ambiguous. drake's one such case was
      dyn drake Unit         ( a Unit dyn: no `=` result to terminate it )
        parameters
          par path String
        mon println(...)
      fix commands ...       <- this dyn's statement, or globals' next member?
  with the [ ] sitting uselessly on the enclosing `globals`. A self-delimiting opener closes it. A `=` result
  line also terminates a dyn, so only Unit dyns ever had the exposure.
  Value-type brackets [T] {T} (A, B) are unaffected — they are inline type expressions, not blocks.
  LEXICAL RULE distinguishing the two: a `[` followed by whitespace opens a block; a `[` glued to what precedes
  or follows it is part of a type or value expression ([T], classOf[Main], the empty-Seq default []).

PRESENCE  (absent vs present-empty vs present-full — the elision principle)
  Every facet and field is in one of three states; preserve the distinction wherever it carries meaning:
    absent         not part of the definition                     -> key omitted
    present-empty  applies, at its default/zero                    -> key emitted, empty/zero value
    present-full   an explicit value / override                   -> key emitted with the value

  ~  ELIDABLE: present-empty == absent. Emptiness says nothing — an empty collection is just "no items";
     a DEFAULT-WHEN-OMITTED field simply takes its default. Empty is omitted from JSON.

  *  (without ~) PRESENCE-SIGNIFICANT: present-empty != absent. Two distinct uses:
       (a) FACET-CREATING — an optional ASPECT whose very presence brings the facet into being.
           rule / actor: present (even empty) = the type IS a rule/actor; absent = not one at all.
       (b) SELECTOR — a field whose presence chooses whether it is generated (codec encoder / decoder).

  Two FIELD behaviours, and they determine what an aspect's present-empty state means:
    DEFAULT-WHEN-OMITTED  an omitted field still generates its default (rule/actor fields; a collection
                          defaults to empty). An aspect whose fields are ALL default-when-omitted is
                          meaningful present-empty (= a fully-defaulted, if trivial, facet)  -> *aspect.
    SELECTOR              an omitted field is SUPPRESSED — generates nothing (codec encoder/decoder). An
                          aspect whose fields are selectors is nothing when present-empty (all suppressed)
                          == absent  -> *~aspect, and absent = the facet is simply not generated.

  The OPTIONAL facets (rule / actor / codec) are OPT-IN: each exists only if its aspect is present, and
  none is computed-by-default — in particular a type with no codec aspect gets no codec. (draco / domain
  are structural and always determinable — see INFERENCE.)

INFERENCE  (authored vs derived — which aspect json must be explicit)
  An aspect is INFERABLE when its json can be reconstructed from information already present; otherwise it
  must be EXPLICIT — carried through verbatim, never conjured.
    rule, actor, codec   NEVER inferred — explicit in BOTH drake and json. They carry irreducible authored
                         intent (a rule's logic, an actor's behaviour, the decision to serialize and how);
                         no structural analysis produces them.
    draco aspect         explicit in drake (the type structure you author); its json is a PROJECTION of
                         that drake authoring (inferred drake -> json).
    domain aspect        INFERABLE from TypeName — its namePackage / name already fix membership
                         (e.g. draco.base.Meters -> the Base domain), so the json domain aspect is derived
                         rather than independently authored.
    reference package    INFERABLE when it is the REFERRING TYPE'S OWN package — a `from` / `modules`
                         reference is spelled bare there and qualified everywhere else (see
                         REFERENCES). Spelling every reference would restate the current package on
                         the 60% of them that are local. A FOREIGN referent has no package to infer
                         and is spelled by its operator instead.
    the universal root   INFERABLE wherever loading would put it back: DracoType is appended to any
                         definition carrying no draco-domain parent, so it is spelled on neither
                         surface — not alone, and not beside a foreign parent (Dictionary). Beside a
                         draco parent it would NOT be restored, and there it is explicit.
    condition params     INFERABLE from the guard and the enclosing pattern: a guard is an EXPRESSION, and the
                         names in it are the pattern's variables — which is what the `variables` block is for.
                         So they are DERIVED AT EMISSION (the mentioned subset, in declaration order, with the
                         types declared there) and stored in neither drake nor json. Not a style choice: the two
                         emissions leave no freedom for them to differ from the variables — `.where` compiles to a
                         Java string whose `$name` resolves only against the `.forEach` fact variables, and w<n>
                         receives exactly what ctx.get[<valueType>] yields, so a disagreement fails Evrete's
                         runtime compile or scalac. A stored copy could only ever agree or be wrong.
  This settles the codec question: codec is DECLARED, never inferred. A type serializes iff it declares a
  codec aspect; the structural "infer a codec for any eligible record" gate (ch.57) is retired.

TEMPLATE

type <name> *(<T>, ...) *from <ref> ...         -> TypeDefinition ; typeName.name , typeName.typeParameters[] ; dracoAspect.derivation[]
                                           ( a SEQUENCE of references, space-separated; see REFERENCES )
  *~modules [ <ref> ... ]                  -> dracoAspect.modules[]
  *~extensible <pkg-seq> <ExtType> *(<T>, ...)  -> dracoAspect.extensible
  *~elements                               -> dracoAspect.elements[]
    fix|mut|dyn <name> <value-type> *<value>   -> { kind=Fixed|Mutable|Dynamic , name , valueType , value }
    ...
  *~factory *<value-type>                  -> dracoAspect.factory   { kind=Factory , valueType }   ( value-type elided = the enclosing type; named only when it differs, e.g. `factory ActorType` )
    ~parameters                            -> factory.parameters[]
      par _<name> <value-type> *<default-value>  -> { kind=Parameter , name , valueType , value }
      ...
    *~body                                 -> factory.body[]
      fix|mut|dyn|loc <name> <value-type> *<value>   -> BodyElement
      ...
  *~globals                                -> dracoAspect.globalElements[]
    fix|mut|dyn <name> <value-type> *<value>   -> BodyElement
    ...
domain <pkg-seq> <name>                    -> domainAspect.typeName { namePackage[] , name }   (namePackage also sets each type's namePackage)
  *~types [ <name> ... ]                   -> domainAspect.elementTypeNames[]   (rule/actor members keep their aspect suffix, e.g. AddNaturalSequence.rule)
  *~super <pkg-seq> <name>                 -> dracoAspect.superDomain   (NOTE: dracoAspect, not domainAspect)
  *~source <pkg-seq> <name>                -> domainAspect.source
  *~target <pkg-seq> <name>                -> domainAspect.target   ( A DIRECTION. Presence of BOTH ends makes the domain a
                                               TRANSFORM domain — role is presence, as everywhere else, so nothing further
                                               marks it and no derivation carries it. One end alone names no conversion and
                                               is not a transform domain. Both are NOMINAL references to DOMAINS, and
                                               neither is inherited: a transform domain holds no value of either side, only
                                               the conversions between them, so the pair is metadata about the domain rather
                                               than its structure. Each MEMBER conversion identifies itself the same way and
                                               needs no keyword: it derives a type in `target` and takes a parameter typed
                                               in `source`, which is the unit-conversion pattern — `Meters from Distance(Double)`
                                               is the same shape with the formula in the factory body. )
*rule                                      -> ruleAspect                    (presence-significant, FACET-CREATING: present = is a rule, every field defaults & is overridable; absent = not a rule)
  *~pattern                                -> ruleAspect.pattern   { kind=Pattern }    ( default when omitted; content overrides )
    *~variables                            -> pattern.variables[]
      var <name> <value-type>              -> { kind=Variable , name , valueType }
      ...
    *~conditions                           -> pattern.conditions[]
      con <bool-value>                     -> { kind=Condition , value }
      ...                                    ( A guard and nothing else — a condition has no other content. WHICH
                                               variables it constrains is not stored anywhere: it is the subset of the
                                               enclosing `variables` the guard MENTIONS, in declaration order, and the
                                               target derives it at emission. ScalaTarget emits
                                               def w<n>(<those>): Boolean and the matching .where("…w<n>($…)") from
                                               that derivation. A name reached through a dot is a selection, not a
                                               reference: td.dracoAspect mentions td. )
  *~action                                 -> ruleAspect.action   { kind=Action }     ( default when omitted; content overrides )
    fix <name> <value-type> <value>        -> action.body[] { kind=Fixed }
    ...
    mon <unit-value>                       -> action.body[] { kind=Monadic , value }
    ...
*actor                                     -> actorAspect                   (presence-significant, FACET-CREATING: present = is an actor, every action defaults & is overridable; absent = not an actor)
  *~messageType *<pkg-seq> <name> *(<T>, ...) -> actorAspect.messageType    ( the actor's T, carried by the ASPECT rather than by an Actor(T) derivation.
                                                                             A REFERENCE like from/modules: bare in the actor's own package, qualified
                                                                             elsewhere — `messageType draco format json JSON`. The target spells it
                                                                             package-relative again, so the definition carries the package and the
                                                                             projection decides whether to write it. )
  *~start                                  -> actorAspect.start             ( default behaviour generated regardless of presence; content overrides )
  *~message                                -> actorAspect.message           ( default behaviour generated regardless of presence; content overrides )
    *~case *<name> <value-type> [ ... ]     -> message.body[] { kind=Case }   ( OPTIONAL — absent = no dispatch; see CASE-BRANCH )
  *~signal                                 -> actorAspect.signal            ( default behaviour generated regardless of presence; content overrides )
*~codec                                    -> codecAspect                   (absent = NO codec for this type. present = a codec is needed; its fields are SELECTORS, so a present codec must carry >=1 of encoder/decoder — never both absent. present-empty == absent)
  *~discriminator <value>                  -> codecAspect.discriminator     ( OPEN: carried from the earlier increment as a String field driving the union tag ("kind"). Field vs derived is unsettled — see NOTES )
  *encoder                                 -> codecAspect.encoder   { kind=Encoder }   ( SELECTOR — present generates the encoder (default or custom); absent SUPPRESSES it )
    *~fields                               -> encoder.fields[]
      enc <name> *as <wire-key> *when <bool-value> *value <value>  -> { kind=EncoderField , name , wireKey , when , value }
      ...
  *decoder                                 -> codecAspect.decoder   { kind=Decoder }   ( SELECTOR — present generates the decoder (default or custom); absent SUPPRESSES it )
    *~fields                               -> decoder.fields[]
      dec <name> *from <wire-key> *default <default-value>         -> { kind=DecoderField , name , wireKey , default }
      ...
  ( CODEC SELECTOR — a present codec generates exactly the sides that are present; an omitted side is
    SUPPRESSED, not defaulted (the difference from rule/actor). At least one side is required:
        no codec aspect (absent)          -> no codec at all
        encoder present, decoder present  -> both
        encoder present, decoder absent   -> encoder only  (decoder suppressed)
        decoder present, encoder absent   -> decoder only  (encoder suppressed)
        both absent inside a codec        -> invalid — that is just "no codec" == absent
    A side present-empty ⇒ its default (one field per factory parameter); present-with-fields ⇒ custom.
    An EncoderField emits `if (when) Some("<wire-key>" -> <value>.asJson) else None`;
    a DecoderField emits `_<name> <- downField("<wire-key>").as[...].getOrElse(<default>)`. )

NOTES  (deferred to the generator transform domains — NOT pending on the procedural draco.DracoGenerator)
  The deep model here stays open until GenScala's members are per-target operation RULES rather than the
  one function they hold today. The procedural draco.DracoGenerator is unchanged for any of it.
  discriminator   DECIDED for now: keep as the existing top-level String field on codecAspect (default
                  "kind"). Per INFERENCE it is not reconstructable from other data (a free tag choice), so
                  it must be explicit — a field is the honest form. No code change; no immediate concern.
  Encoder/Decoder subelement types (Encoder, Decoder, EncoderField, DecoderField) — DEFERRED. No custom
  override codecs are needed yet; the procedural Generator keeps deriving the default both-sided codec.
  Their shape (as/when/value, from/default) is a sketch for the generator domains, not a current commitment.

DIVERGENCES  (drake surface / target vs current JSON + procedural Generator)
  The target rows below (PRESENCE, CODEC-declared) belong to the generator transform domains once they carry
  rules; the procedural draco.DracoGenerator implements only the current behaviour noted on the right, and is not being changed for them.
  par _<name>                       <->   current JSON stores the name without _ (Generator prepends); target keeps _ in the name
  CASE-BRANCH (this template)       <->   SPEC ONLY — `case` is not yet a reserved word, no Case kind exists, and
                                          neither Drake.emit nor Drake.parse carries one. Reserving the token is the
                                          gating step and it is NOT free: the reserved set is flat and global (Drake.scala),
                                          which is what whitespace-insignificance rests on, so a value span stops at the
                                          FIRST reserved token anywhere. Two pinned definitions carry `case` inside
                                          host-opaque text and would break — TypeName.equals (`other match { case tn: ... }`)
                                          and DefinitionPath.source (`sources(rp) match { case Seq() ... }`).
                                          DefinitionPath's Seq()/Seq(url) is structural ARITY, not subtype dispatch, and
                                          converts to the `if` operator (DONE). TypeName.equals converts to a case-branch
                                          with a default — `case tn TypeName [ = ... ]` / `case [ = false ]` — which
                                          needs the Case kind to exist, so it lands with the kind, as its first use.
  PRESENCE model (this template)    <->   current Generator/JSON still ELIDE uniformly: TypeDefinition's encoder
                                          omits an aspect via X.isEmpty, and every field elides on empty. The template
                                          above is the target — preserve presence for *; elide for ~ — not yet built.
                                          In particular rule/actor are NOT yet presence-significant in the impl
                                          (a present-empty rule currently elides to absent), and the codec selector /
                                          suppress-vs-default distinction is not implemented.
  CODEC declared, not inferred      <->   CONFIRMED direction (see INFERENCE): codec is never inferred. Current Generator
    (target — future migration)           does the opposite — it INFERS a default codec for any eligible record (>=1 own
                                          element, via the ownElementNames gate + inheritedElementNames walk, ch.57).
                                          Migration: retire that gate; add an explicit `codec` aspect to every type that
                                          today auto-derives one (TypeName, the aspects, the TypeElement family, ...).
                                          Scoped separately; not started.
  host-reserved domain names        <->   a domain named `scala` or `java` is INVALID for the JVM target: Scala resolves
                                          packages relatively, and classpath DIRECTORIES (even a resource dir like
                                          draco/scala/) materialize the package, shadowing the real root scala/java for
                                          the whole enclosing package tree. An emission-side escape cannot fix this:
                                          the domain convention couples the package element to the type name
                                          case-insensitively (scala <-> Scala), so escaping the package half alone breaks
                                          the domain invariant and escaping both mangles every type reference.
                                          RESOLUTION: name the capability, not the host language — scalatarget.ScalaTarget,
                                          haskellsource.HaskellSource. A definition-time lint rejecting host-reserved
                                          domain names (per target) is the enforcement; queued with the Monadic lint.
  dyn result: `= <expr>` line       <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-09-13) — and not as the trailing-expression form this entry
    (target — no `=` at all)              once anticipated. A dyn's [ ] contains `parameters` and a `body` section, as a
                                          factory's does, and the RESULT is the body element NAMED `value` (`fix value
                                          <type> <expr>`), the convention factory bodies already used to set an instance's
                                          value. Found by name, so nothing has to be last and no expression grammar is
                                          needed to bound it; the `=` marker is gone from the surface and the parser. See
                                          DYN-WITH-BODY. The named-argument marker `par = <name>` used `=` until the call
                                          syntax landed (2026-09-16, below); no bare `=` is left on the surface.
  BRACKETS on the opener            <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-08-05, GitHub #52). Both directions agree: Generator.drake
                                          emits it and Generator.parseDrake reads it, gated by DrakeParseTest's surface
                                          round-trip. The earlier rule bracketed the CONTAINER, which left one genuine
                                          ambiguity in the corpus (CLI's Unit `dyn drake` before `fix commands`) and forced
                                          the parser onto an indentation tie-break; moving the brackets to the opener
                                          removed it, and drake is now whitespace-insignificant in fact, not just in aim.
  Map as a TYPE EXPRESSION          <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-08-10). `Map[K, V]` in a valueType became `{K, V}` in BOTH
                                          drake and json, and the Scala spelling moved to the target alone
                                          (Generator.scalaTypeExpression, applied by targetTypes at the generate
                                          entry beside TypeLoader.rooted). Six valueTypes, across Dictionary /
                                          TypeDictionary / DomainDictionary / CLI.
                                          THE DERIVATION FOLLOWED (2026-08-25, GitHub #51). Dictionary now reads
                                          `from {K, V}`, one concept spelled one way in one file. `derivation`
                                          still holds TypeNames — a reference is nominal, and flattening 76
                                          qualified parents to text would lose what namePackage already models —
                                          so what changed is the SPELLING: a package-less reference is FOREIGN
                                          (REFERENCES), and a foreign referent is spelled by its operator. With no
                                          package to lose the last round-trip loss closed, and DrakeParseTest now
                                          verifies reference packages outright instead of normalizing them away.
                                          Rooting followed the same seam: TypeLoader.rooted APPENDS the universal
                                          root to a definition with no draco parent rather than gating on an empty
                                          derivation, so Dictionary stopped spelling DracoType by hand and the JSON
                                          corpus is now uniformly implicit with no exception left.
  the ARGUMENT as an opener         <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-08-10, GitHub #60), then RETIRED with the call syntax
                                          (2026-09-16). The second opener under the one rule: a `par` whose value is an
                                          application bracketed itself, which closed the last ambiguity on the surface
                                          (inner and outer argument list admit the same `par`) and let DrakeParseTest
                                          gate the mods actors. Once a call is one token its parentheses bound it, and
                                          the bracket, the `par` list and the chain lines all went.
  the CALL `f(a, b)`                <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-09-16; agreed 2026-09-13). A call is written as every
    (was `<fn> parameters` / `par`)       reader expects — positional arguments, then `name:value`, defaults from the
                                          declaration — and parsed by splitting the one glued token at depth-0
                                          parentheses, commas and dots (APPLICATION SURFACE). The multi-line form went
                                          with its brackets and chain lines; 13 corpus files converted. CONSEQUENCE: the
                                          parser now trees EVERY parenthesised call, including the ~105 host-opaque
                                          strings the JSON corpus still carries for calls, so parse(emit(td)) returns
                                          trees where the JSON has strings — DrakeParseTest's expression-form loss
                                          rises until the JSON is re-canonicalized from the drake (next increment:
                                          `DrakeCLI parse X.drake > X.json`, Scala pinned by DracoGenTest).
                                          DONE the same day (8acd9c7): 40 JSON files, Value held back under #61.
  valueType as a JSON NODE          <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-09-16). The TypeElement family, Factory, Variable and
    (was a String)                        Case declare `valueType Json`, decoded absent-as-Null and elided
                                          null-or-empty exactly as `value` is; the corpus stays strings, so no
                                          definition and no generated line moved except the family's own and
                                          DracoAspect's emptiness test (`.isNull`). Consumers read
                                          `.valueType.text` (draco.TypeForm, beside Expression); the engine spells
                                          the type for the target once, in targetTypes. NEXT: the parser trees the
                                          four forms (VALUE-TYPES above) and the corpus re-canonicalizes from the
                                          drake; then typeParameters, which lives in TypeName's identity.
  lazy by default, `now`            <->   STEP 1 IMPLEMENTED (2026-09-17): a fix / loc inside a method, action or
    (EVALUATION)                          factory body renders `lazy val`; `now` before it keeps `val`. TypeElement
                                          gains `now Boolean false` (the binding kinds' factories take it last, so
                                          no caller moves; the Boolean elision rule is new — a false elides). Parser
                                          and emitter carry the prefix; DrakeParseTest asserts it.
                                          STEP 2 (2026-09-18): a method parameter is by-name, `_x: => T`, bound once
                                          by a `lazy val x: T = _x` prelude the body opens with; abstract methods
                                          carry the same signature, so `fold`'s `absent` is lazy and a Present never
                                          evaluates it (PresenceTest). First real `now`: TypeName's `equals(other)`
                                          meets the host's by-value signature.
                                          STEP 3 (2026-09-18): a factory parameter is by-name, `_x: => T`, and the
                                          factory body is its prelude — a bodiless factory binds each parameter
                                          once as the instance's `override lazy val x = _x`, a bodied one reads
                                          `_x` inside the lazy members it authors — so `Present(x)` constructs
                                          without evaluating `x` (PresenceTest). `now par` keeps a parameter
                                          strict; the corpus needs it nowhere.
                                          STEP 4 (2026-09-18): the actor-minting factory follows — `def actorType
                                          (_consumer: => ActorRef[M])` with `lazy val consumer = _consumer` as the
                                          first member of the minted actor, read by its actions per message. The
                                          four steps close EVALUATION: every parameter, binding and method in the
                                          corpus is call-by-need, and `now` marks the 8 the host forces.
                                          First consequence seen: a CALLER sequences its effects — Drake.parse
                                          passed cursor reads as arguments and they ran at first read, out of
                                          token order, until bound before the call (133 tests, one run).
                                          A bodied factory that reads a parameter TWICE authors the prelude
                                          itself — `loc x T _x`, then reads x — or reads the member its first
                                          line bound (Domain, TypeDictionary, DomainLineOf, 2026-09-18).
  Presence(T) / Present / Absent    <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-09-17) — draco.drake's first members, the runtime's own
    (draco's option, not host Option)     sum type: `Presence(T)` with `modules [Present, Absent]`, `Present(T)` carrying
                                          `par value T`, `Absent(T)` bare and parameterized (variance is the later
                                          concept). The first PARAMETERIZED sealed family the generator emits, and it
                                          carries NO host codec: a discriminated union over Presence[T] would need an
                                          Encoder[T] the companion cannot name, so a parameterized family is codec-less
                                          as a parameterized plain type (Value(F)) already is — Presence's codec is the
                                          CODEC ASPECT's business, in drake. A Null whose parameter is typed by the
                                          type's own variable instantiates at Any, not Nothing.
                                          `fold` LANDED the same day: `dyn fold(R) R` with `absent R` and `present
                                          (T -> R)`, abstract on Presence, `present(value)` in Present, `absent` in
                                          Absent — the eliminator as DISPATCH, in drake, no host code, no conditional;
                                          the first member override of a parent dyn. Call surface `p.fold(absent, f)`.
                                          `ifThenElse` LANDED 2026-09-20 as the SECOND dyn: `dyn ifThenElse(R) R`
                                          with `thenBranch R` / `elseBranch R`, abstract on Presence, `thenBranch`
                                          in Present, `elseBranch` in Absent — dispatch, no keyword, both branches
                                          by need so a recursion through it terminates (PresenceTest). A Boolean
                                          reaches it through `guard` (SYMBOLS); TypeLoader.rooted is the first use.
                                          TypeName's two `if`s DISSOLVED into joins (2026-09-20): the empty path is a
                                          module like any other, so `join(".", namePackage ++ [name])` spells every
                                          namePath and `"/" ++ join("/", namePackage ++ [name ++ ".json"])` every
                                          resourcePath; the special case, and the two interpolated host strings, are
                                          gone. DefinitionPath's two sit inside a lambda and wait on GitHub #61.
  the four TYPE FORMS as trees      <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-09-17). Drake.typeForm reads a value-type slot to a
    (valueType was host text)             tree; Drake emits one back (drakeType); DracoGenerator.targetType spells
                                          it for Scala (`F[A]`, `(A, B)`, `S => T`, a Morphic left operand
                                          parenthesized). No corpus JSON moved in the commit — the re-canonicalization
                                          from the drake follows with the compiled parser, as the call syntax's did —
                                          so DrakeParseTest's report-only loss gains a "type form" category until it
                                          lands. The `mut {T}` tail stays a string leaf. (The corpus landed in the
                                          same commit, f4113d2: ScenarioGenTest asserts json == parse(drake).)
  typeParameters as JSON NODES      <->   IMPLEMENTED (2026-09-17). `TypeName.typeParameters` is `[Json]` on the
    (was [String])                        value-type terms; the corpus stays strings (`T`, `T <: Product`, `(S, T)`),
                                          TypeName's structural identity compares the nodes, the engine spells each
                                          at its render sites through targetType (TypeName is identity, so
                                          targetTypes does not rewrite it in place), the surface spells a string
                                          verbatim and a tree as any type form. THE PARSER TREES THEM (2026-09-17,
                                          same day): header parameters, `from` / `modules` / `domain` / `super` /
                                          `source` / `target` / `messageType` / `extensible` arguments, and a
                                          foreign reference's members, all through typeForm. Four src/main
                                          definitions carry a compound or bounded parameter (DomainTransform,
                                          TypeTransform, Holon, base/Coordinate); their JSON re-canonicalizes
                                          with the compiled parser next, as the value types' did.
